1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,160
This is ECE 320, video 17 c, a midterm review. It covers the spring 2020 quiz 2, and one method for each question type.

2
00:00:12,610 --> 00:00:17,111
Question 1 calculates the CPI with a memory hierarchy.

3
00:00:17,561 --> 00:00:26,116
Question 2 runs a loop with a predicted-taken branch, then fills a two-bit counter, B H T and B T B table.

4
00:00:26,566 --> 00:00:34,758
Question 3 is a design question on interrupts: save the P C, shadow registers, and the context in the cache.

5
00:00:35,208 --> 00:00:42,269
At the end, one method card for each midterm question type gives the steps and the common mistakes.

6
00:00:42,719 --> 00:00:47,989
The spring 2020 quiz 2 has 6 questions and 84 marks.

7
00:00:48,439 --> 00:00:55,713
Questions 1 to 3 carry 42 marks. All three use lecture slides 1 to 8, so we solve them.

8
00:00:56,163 --> 00:01:08,110
Question 4 is out-of-order execution. Questions 5 and 6 are memory consistency and cache coherence. They are not on slides 1 to 8, so we skip them.

9
00:01:08,560 --> 00:01:17,371
Each question starts with the full question. Then pause the video and try it. Then follow the solution, and check the one-line answer.

10
00:01:17,821 --> 00:01:34,717
Spring 2020 quiz 2, question 1. For memory instructions, 60 percent hit in L 1, at 5 cycles. 20 percent hit in L 2, at 10 cycles. 20 percent go to local memory, at 300 cycles.

11
00:01:35,167 --> 00:01:44,809
The other instructions: arithmetic and logical instructions are 50 percent, at 4 cycles. Branches are 10 percent, at 2 cycles.

12
00:01:45,259 --> 00:01:55,414
Part A asks for the CPI of the memory instructions. Part B asks for the overall CPI. Pause the video and try parts A and B.

13
00:01:55,864 --> 00:02:03,715
Assume that each latency is the total time of an access served at that level. State the assumption in the exam.

14
00:02:04,165 --> 00:02:13,402
Part A is a weighted average over the levels. The strip shows where the memory instructions are served: 60, 20 and 20 percent.

15
00:02:13,852 --> 00:02:19,356
L 1 serves 60 percent at 5 cycles. Point 6 times 5 is 3.

16
00:02:19,806 --> 00:02:24,990
L 2 serves 20 percent at 10 cycles. Point 2 times 10 is 2.

17
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:32,160
Local memory serves 20 percent at 300 cycles. Point 2 times 300 is 60.

18
00:02:32,610 --> 00:02:43,383
Add the parts. 3 plus 2 plus 60 is 65. So the CPI of the memory instructions is 65. Memory dominates the bar.

19
00:02:43,833 --> 00:02:57,273
Part B. Arithmetic and logical instructions are 50 percent, and branches are 10 percent. Memory instructions are the rest: 100 minus 50 minus 10 is 40 percent.

20
00:02:57,723 --> 00:03:00,582
Arithmetic: point 5 times 4 is 2.

21
00:03:01,032 --> 00:03:03,976
Branches: point 1 times 2 is point 2.

22
00:03:04,426 --> 00:03:10,186
Memory: point 4 times 65, the CPI from part A, is 26.

23
00:03:10,636 --> 00:03:16,567
The overall CPI is 2 plus point 2 plus 26, which is 28.2.

24
00:03:17,017 --> 00:03:26,553
The answer. Part A: the CPI of the memory instructions is 65. Part B: the overall CPI is 28.2.

25
00:03:27,003 --> 00:03:34,192
Part C, 4 marks. An optimization makes arithmetic and logical instructions 2 times faster.

26
00:03:34,642 --> 00:03:42,706
But half of the memory instructions that went to local memory now go to remote memory, at 450 cycles.

27
00:03:43,156 --> 00:03:47,657
The question: does program P benefit. Pause the video and try it.

28
00:03:48,107 --> 00:03:53,803
First, the new memory CPI. L 1 and L 2 do not change: 3 and 2.

29
00:03:54,253 --> 00:04:01,635
Half of the 20 percent local accesses stay local: 10 percent. Point 1 times 300 is 30.

30
00:04:02,085 --> 00:04:08,741
The other 10 percent go to remote memory. Point 1 times 450 is 45.

31
00:04:09,191 --> 00:04:16,871
The memory CPI is 3 plus 2 plus 30 plus 45, which is 80. Before, it was 65.

32
00:04:17,321 --> 00:04:26,259
Now the overall CPI. Arithmetic takes 4 divided by 2, which is 2 cycles. Point 5 times 2 is 1.

33
00:04:26,709 --> 00:04:30,656
Branches do not change. Point 1 times 2 is point 2.

34
00:04:31,106 --> 00:04:34,029
Memory: point 4 times 80 is 32.

35
00:04:34,479 --> 00:04:39,983
The new CPI is 1 plus point 2 plus 32, which is 33.2.

36
00:04:40,433 --> 00:04:44,507
The old CPI, from parts A and B, is 28.2.

37
00:04:44,957 --> 00:04:49,309
The arithmetic part saves 2 minus 1, which is 1 cycle.

38
00:04:49,759 --> 00:04:54,794
But the memory part adds 32 minus 26, which is 6 cycles.

39
00:04:55,244 --> 00:05:04,780
The instruction count and the clock do not change. 33.2 is more than 28.2, so program P does not benefit.

40
00:05:05,230 --> 00:05:12,675
It is slower: the speedup is 28.2 divided by 33.2, which is point 8 5.

41
00:05:13,125 --> 00:05:23,067
Question 2 part A. Run program Q on the 5-stage pipeline. There is bypassing, but not into D, and not into the branch compare unit.

42
00:05:23,517 --> 00:05:30,557
The branch, blt, is predicted taken, but it resolves not taken. Pause the video and try it.

43
00:05:31,007 --> 00:05:37,727
Add needs t 0 from the load. That is a load-use stall: add waits in D in cycle 3.

44
00:05:38,177 --> 00:05:44,811
In cycle 5, the load is in W. W X forwarding gives t 0 to add in X.

45
00:05:45,261 --> 00:05:47,651
The branch is fetched in cycle 5.

46
00:05:48,101 --> 00:05:56,549
The branch needs a 0 from add immediate, with no forwarding. Add immediate writes a 0 in W, in cycle 8.

47
00:05:56,999 --> 00:06:04,401
D reads only values that were written in earlier cycles. So the branch reads a 0 in D in cycle 9.

48
00:06:04,851 --> 00:06:10,377
The predictor says taken. So from cycle 6, F fetches the target, hex 10.

49
00:06:10,827 --> 00:06:15,925
In cycle 10, the branch resolves in X: not taken. The guess was wrong.

50
00:06:16,375 --> 00:06:20,237
The wrong load and add become dashes, from cycle 11.

51
00:06:20,687 --> 00:06:24,441
In cycle 11, F fetches the nop at hex 20.

52
00:06:24,891 --> 00:06:27,153
The nop finishes in cycle 15.

53
00:06:27,603 --> 00:06:30,525
But the quiz table has only 14 columns.

54
00:06:30,975 --> 00:06:41,151
It fits if the register file writes, then reads, in the same cycle. Then the branch reads a 0 in D in cycle 8, the cycle of the write.

55
00:06:41,601 --> 00:06:49,815
Every later cell moves one cycle earlier, so the nop finishes in cycle 14. State your assumption in the exam.

56
00:06:50,265 --> 00:07:10,147
The answer. Add stalls once for the load, then uses W X. blt waits in D for add immediate. The wrong-path load and add are flushed, and the nop is fetched in the cycle after X. The last W is in cycle 15, or 14 with a same-cycle write and read.

57
00:07:10,597 --> 00:07:18,619
Part B adds a 2-bit counter B H T and a B T B. Pause the video and fill the table for blt.

58
00:07:19,069 --> 00:07:22,183
States 1 0 and 1 1 predict taken.

59
00:07:22,633 --> 00:07:27,583
a 0 starts at hex 1000, and a 1 is hex 1010.

60
00:07:28,033 --> 00:07:40,406
Add immediate adds 4 to a 0 before each blt. So blt sees hex 1004, 1008 and 100 C. Each is less than a 1: taken.

61
00:07:40,856 --> 00:07:48,664
Then blt sees hex 1010, which equals a 1: not taken. So blt runs 4 times.

62
00:07:49,114 --> 00:07:57,839
Run 1 is given. The entry 0 1 predicts not taken, but blt is taken. So the entry moves to 1 0.

63
00:07:58,289 --> 00:08:04,476
A taken branch writes the B T B. So the B T B now holds the target, hex 10.

64
00:08:04,926 --> 00:08:13,203
Run 2: the entry is 1 0. It predicts taken, with the target hex 10. Correct, so the entry moves to 1 1.

65
00:08:13,653 --> 00:08:18,816
Run 3: 1 1 predicts taken. Correct, and the entry stays at 1 1.

66
00:08:19,266 --> 00:08:30,231
Run 4: 1 1 predicts taken, but blt is not taken. The entry moves to 1 0. A not-taken branch does not write the B T B.

67
00:08:30,681 --> 00:08:36,996
The last row is the state after the loop: entry 1 0, predict taken, target hex 10.

68
00:08:37,446 --> 00:08:42,459
Rows 5 and 6 stay blank, because blt runs only 4 times.

69
00:08:42,909 --> 00:08:59,123
The answer. The entry goes 0 1, 1 0, 1 1, 1 1. The predictions are not taken, then taken 3 times. The outcomes are taken 3 times, then not taken. The last row is 1 0, taken, hex 10.

70
00:08:59,573 --> 00:09:09,685
Now the spring 2020 quiz, question 3. Interrupts happen at a high frequency, so context saving and restoring must be fast.

71
00:09:10,135 --> 00:09:18,839
The context is the 31 registers x 1 to x 31. x 0 is always 0, so it is not saved.

72
00:09:19,289 --> 00:09:22,297
Normally, the handler saves them in main memory.

73
00:09:22,747 --> 00:09:27,099
This quiz resolves interrupts and exceptions at the W stage.

74
00:09:27,549 --> 00:09:33,330
The slides use M. The method is the same: one commit point, and act only there.

75
00:09:33,780 --> 00:09:37,193
There are five parts. Parts A and B have no cache.

76
00:09:37,643 --> 00:09:42,955
Part C adds a data cache. It uses the cache ideas from video 14.

77
00:09:43,405 --> 00:09:52,216
Part A. With no cache, describe one way to save and restore the P C when an interrupt happens. Pause the video and try it.

78
00:09:52,666 --> 00:10:00,303
Follow the slides. First, carry the P C of each instruction down the pipeline, in every pipeline register.

79
00:10:00,753 --> 00:10:05,703
Carry it up to the commit point. In this quiz, the commit point is W.

80
00:10:06,153 --> 00:10:11,145
At W, write E P C with the P C of the interrupted instruction.

81
00:10:11,595 --> 00:10:13,707
Also write Cause with the reason.

82
00:10:14,157 --> 00:10:17,847
Kill the younger instructions in F, D, X and M.

83
00:10:18,297 --> 00:10:21,412
The instruction in W does not write back.

84
00:10:21,862 --> 00:10:24,657
Then the P C select picks the handler address.

85
00:10:25,107 --> 00:10:35,304
To restore, add a return instruction at the end of the handler. It copies E P C into the P C. Fetch restarts at the interrupted instruction.

86
00:10:35,754 --> 00:10:44,117
Part B adds two new instructions. Save context saves all 31 registers. Load context restores them.

87
00:10:44,567 --> 00:10:48,663
The register file gets two sets, set 0 and set 1.

88
00:10:49,113 --> 00:10:52,761
A bit s selects the current set. Now s is 0.

89
00:10:53,211 --> 00:10:59,205
The copy operation copies the current set s into the other set, s X O R 1.

90
00:10:59,655 --> 00:11:06,077
Now set 1 holds the same values as set 0: 5, 9, and so on, to 7.

91
00:11:06,527 --> 00:11:14,292
The swap operation changes s to s X O R 1. Now s is 1, so set 1 is the current set.

92
00:11:14,742 --> 00:11:19,905
If both come in the same cycle, the register file copies first, then swaps.

93
00:11:20,355 --> 00:11:23,533
This design does not support nested interrupts.

94
00:11:23,983 --> 00:11:28,634
We use the copy for save context, and the swap for load context.

95
00:11:29,084 --> 00:11:36,913
Part B 1. How does an application benefit from save context and load context? Pause the video and try it.

96
00:11:37,363 --> 00:11:43,827
Without them, the handler saves the context with 31 store words, one for each register.

97
00:11:44,277 --> 00:11:48,437
At the end, it restores the context with 31 load words.

98
00:11:48,887 --> 00:11:54,349
31 plus 31 is 62 memory instructions for each interrupt.

99
00:11:54,799 --> 00:12:00,153
With them, the handler uses 2 instructions: save context and load context.

100
00:12:00,603 --> 00:12:06,001
The copy and the swap happen inside the register file, with no memory access.

101
00:12:06,451 --> 00:12:13,341
So each interrupt costs much less time. This matters, because interrupts come at a high frequency.

102
00:12:13,791 --> 00:12:23,242
Part B 2. Which changes does the pipeline need, apart from the register file? Go stage by stage. Pause the video and try it.

103
00:12:23,692 --> 00:12:28,428
F needs no change. The new instructions are fetched like all others.

104
00:12:28,878 --> 00:12:42,915
D decodes the two new opcodes. It makes two new control bits: copy for save context, and swap for load context. The instructions write no register, so Reg W En is 0.

105
00:12:43,365 --> 00:12:51,088
X and M do no work. They only carry the two bits down the pipeline registers, like Reg W En.

106
00:12:51,538 --> 00:12:56,381
W is the commit point, so the copy or swap happens in W.

107
00:12:56,831 --> 00:13:09,225
All older instructions have written their results, so the copy is complete. And if an interrupt kills save context, it has changed nothing. Younger instructions have not written yet.

108
00:13:09,675 --> 00:13:19,958
One hazard remains. After load context swaps the sets, the younger instructions in D, X and M have read registers from the old set.

109
00:13:20,408 --> 00:13:27,448
Flush F, D, X and M, and fetch again after load context. This is like a wrong branch guess.

110
00:13:27,898 --> 00:13:37,093
Part B 3. The change breaks some existing interrupt handlers. Describe the problem, and how to fix it. Pause the video and try it.

111
00:13:37,543 --> 00:13:44,284
Trace the two sets. P means the program registers. At the start, the program uses set 0.

112
00:13:44,734 --> 00:13:50,729
Interrupt 1 runs save context. It is a copy, so set 1 also holds P.

113
00:13:51,179 --> 00:13:55,765
Handler 1 changes set 0. Set 0 now holds H 1.

114
00:13:56,215 --> 00:14:10,999
Now an existing handler enables interrupts again, and a nested interrupt comes. Handler 2 runs save context. The copy writes H 1 into set 1. The saved program registers P are lost.

115
00:14:11,449 --> 00:14:14,308
Handler 2 changes set 0 to H 2.

116
00:14:14,758 --> 00:14:23,206
Handler 2 runs load context. The swap makes s equal to 1. Handler 1 gets H 1 back, which is correct.

117
00:14:23,656 --> 00:14:33,512
Then handler 1 runs load context. It swaps back to set 0. The program resumes with the registers of handler 2. That is wrong.

118
00:14:33,962 --> 00:14:45,589
The fix: keep interrupts disabled from save context to load context. A handler that must allow nested interrupts uses store word and load word, as before.

119
00:14:46,039 --> 00:14:59,692
A second problem: load context restores all 31 registers. So a handler can no longer return a result in a register, for example after e call. It must return it through memory.

120
00:15:00,142 --> 00:15:11,150
Part C adds a data cache. Acquire context reserves an address range for the context. The lines of the range stay in the cache until release context.

121
00:15:11,600 --> 00:15:20,368
Part C uses the cache ideas from video 14: blocks, sets, tags, and L R U. Pause the video and try it.

122
00:15:20,818 --> 00:15:29,479
First, the size. Each register has 4 bytes. 31 registers times 4 bytes is 124 bytes.

123
00:15:29,929 --> 00:15:36,009
The quiz gives no block size. Assume 32 byte blocks, as in the lecture example.

124
00:15:36,459 --> 00:15:44,907
Take r s 1 equal to hex 1 0 0 0, a multiple of 32. Then the range fills 4 lines.

125
00:15:45,357 --> 00:15:52,653
Each line holds 32 over 4, so 8 registers. The first line holds x 1 to x 8.

126
00:15:53,103 --> 00:15:59,375
The last line holds x 25 to x 31. Its last 4 bytes are unused.

127
00:15:59,825 --> 00:16:07,121
Now take r s 1 equal to hex 1 0 1 0. The range starts 16 bytes into a line.

128
00:16:07,571 --> 00:16:10,046
Now the range touches 5 lines.

129
00:16:10,496 --> 00:16:20,501
So a start more than 4 bytes into a line gives 5 lines. The hardware must handle every line from the first to the last address of the range.

130
00:16:20,951 --> 00:16:25,175
Now the pipeline, stage by stage. F needs no change.

131
00:16:25,625 --> 00:16:35,759
D decodes the two new opcodes and reads r s 1. It makes two control bits, acquire and release. The instructions write no register.

132
00:16:36,209 --> 00:16:43,718
X computes the start address. The A L U passes r s 1, as for a load word with offset 0.

133
00:16:44,168 --> 00:16:54,643
M is the main change: the instructions use the data cache in M. Acquire fills each line of the range and locks it. Release unlocks the lines.

134
00:16:55,093 --> 00:16:59,850
A fill is a cache miss, so the pipeline stalls, as for a load word miss.

135
00:17:00,300 --> 00:17:04,268
W does nothing. The instructions write no register.

136
00:17:04,718 --> 00:17:11,694
To save the context, the handler still uses 31 store words, and later 31 load words.

137
00:17:12,144 --> 00:17:18,267
They access the data cache in M, and they always hit. The locked lines never leave the cache.

138
00:17:18,717 --> 00:17:22,834
Acquire context runs only once, when the program starts.

139
00:17:23,284 --> 00:17:28,895
Now the cache. Take a 2 way set associative cache: way 0 and way 1.

140
00:17:29,345 --> 00:17:32,822
The cache has 4 sets, set 0 to set 3.

141
00:17:33,272 --> 00:17:39,928
Each frame has a valid bit V, a tag, and a 32 byte block, as in video 14.

142
00:17:40,378 --> 00:17:45,306
Add one new bit to every frame: a lock bit, L. It starts at 0.

143
00:17:45,756 --> 00:17:54,247
Acquire context goes through each line of the range. On a miss, it fills the line from memory. Then it sets L to 1.

144
00:17:54,697 --> 00:18:04,467
The set comes from the address bits. A 32 byte block gives 5 offset bits, bits 4 to 0, because 32 is 2 to the 5.

145
00:18:04,917 --> 00:18:15,499
The 4 sets give 2 index bits, bits 6 and 5, because 4 is 2 to the 2. This is the same as the line address over 32, mod 4.

146
00:18:15,949 --> 00:18:30,818
The lines start at hex 1 0 0 0, 32 bytes apart. So their index bits count 0 0, 0 1, 1 0, 1 1. The 4 lines fall into sets 0, 1, 2 and 3.

147
00:18:31,268 --> 00:18:37,924
So acquire context fills way 0 of sets 0 to 3, and sets L to 1 in each frame.

148
00:18:38,374 --> 00:18:48,699
The replacement policy, for example L R U, must never pick a frame with L equal to 1. So a locked line stays in the cache until release.

149
00:18:49,149 --> 00:18:55,912
One more check. Each set needs at least one unlocked way. Way 1 stays free for the other blocks.

150
00:18:56,362 --> 00:19:05,279
In a direct mapped cache, each set has only one way. The other blocks of a locked set cannot be cached, so they must go to memory.

151
00:19:05,729 --> 00:19:17,633
Also, take r s 1 equal to hex 1 0 1 0. The range touches 5 lines, from hex 1 0 0 0 to hex 1 0 8 0.

152
00:19:18,083 --> 00:19:27,854
Lines hex 1 0 0 0 and hex 1 0 8 0 both have index bits 0 0. So both map to set 0.

153
00:19:28,304 --> 00:19:32,976
So both ways of set 0 lock, and set 0 has no free way.

154
00:19:33,426 --> 00:19:36,903
So r s 1 must be a multiple of 32.

155
00:19:37,353 --> 00:19:42,900
Release context sets L back to 0. The line becomes a normal block again.

156
00:19:43,350 --> 00:19:49,345
In a write back cache, the line is written to memory when it is replaced, if it is dirty.

157
00:19:49,795 --> 00:20:06,392
Here are the answers of question 3 in one view. Part A: carry each P C down to W. At W, write E P C and Cause, kill the younger instructions, and fetch the handler. A return instruction copies E P C back.

158
00:20:06,842 --> 00:20:17,253
Part B 1: two instructions with no memory access replace 31 store words and 31 load words. So each interrupt is much faster.

159
00:20:17,703 --> 00:20:31,719
Part B 2: D decodes the new instructions and makes the copy and swap bits. X and M carry the bits. W does the copy or the swap. Flush the younger instructions after load context.

160
00:20:32,169 --> 00:20:44,542
Part B 3: a nested save context overwrites the saved set, so the program resumes with wrong registers. Keep interrupts off from save context to load context.

161
00:20:44,992 --> 00:21:03,445
Part C: D decodes and reads r s 1, X passes the address, and M fills and locks the lines. The cache gets a lock bit per frame. L R U skips locked lines, each set keeps a free way, and r s 1 is aligned to 32 bytes.

162
00:21:03,895 --> 00:21:10,765
Method card 1: Amdahl's law and CPU time. Draw the old time as a bar of length 1.

163
00:21:11,215 --> 00:21:20,025
Mark the part f that the change speeds up. Here f is point 6 of the old time. The rest, 1 minus f, is point 4.

164
00:21:20,475 --> 00:21:28,817
Divide that part by its speedup. Point 6 over 3 is point 2. A part that gets slower is multiplied instead.

165
00:21:29,267 --> 00:21:42,877
Add the parts. The new time is point 4 plus point 2, which is point 6. The speedup is 1 over point 6, which is 1.67. The slide formula gives the same result.

166
00:21:43,327 --> 00:21:55,530
For CPU time, use I C times CPI times the clock period. Change only the factors that the question changes. The CPI of a mix is a weighted sum.

167
00:21:55,980 --> 00:22:06,348
Common mistakes. f is a part of the time, not of the instructions. Keep the part that does not change. And a speedup below 1 means slower: say so.

168
00:22:06,798 --> 00:22:17,827
Method card 2: the hazard pairs RAW, WAR and WAW. First, for each instruction, write the register it writes and the registers it reads.

169
00:22:18,277 --> 00:22:35,216
RAW means read after write. A younger instruction reads a register that an older one writes. Take the most recent older writer. Here: 1 2 on x 1, 2 3 on x 3, 2 4 on x 3, and 3 4 on x 1.

170
00:22:35,666 --> 00:22:46,738
Instruction 4 reads x 1 from instruction 3, not from instruction 1. Instruction 3 writes x 1 between them, so 1 4 is not a pair.

171
00:22:47,188 --> 00:22:58,964
WAW means write after write: 1 and 3 both write x 1. WAR means write after read: 2 reads x 1, and then 3 writes x 1.

172
00:22:59,414 --> 00:23:09,675
In the 5-stage pipeline, reads happen in D and writes in W, in program order. So only RAW needs forwarding or a stall.

173
00:23:10,125 --> 00:23:22,051
Common mistakes. Do not count a pair when a writer comes between them. A store writes no register; its data register is a read. And x 0 never makes a hazard.

174
00:23:22,501 --> 00:23:35,877
Method card 3: pipeline diagram rules. One row per instruction, one column per cycle. Each instruction goes F, D, X, M, W, one stage per cycle, in order.

175
00:23:36,327 --> 00:23:46,225
An A L U result exists at the end of X. A load result exists at the end of M. Here the load has its value at the end of cycle 4.

176
00:23:46,675 --> 00:23:58,067
Add needs x 1 in X. Cycle 4 is too early, so add stalls in D. The repeat gets a star: D star in cycle 3, then D in cycle 4.

177
00:23:58,517 --> 00:24:05,941
The store behind it cannot enter D, so it repeats F: F star in cycle 3, then F in cycle 4.

178
00:24:06,391 --> 00:24:14,562
In cycle 5, the load is in W and add is in X. W X forwarding gives x 1 to the A L U.

179
00:24:15,012 --> 00:24:27,129
Store data uses only W M. In cycle 7, add is in W and the store is in M. There is no forwarding into D, and branches resolve in X.

180
00:24:27,579 --> 00:24:40,614
Common mistakes. A load has no M X path. A D in the same cycle as the producer's W reads the old value, so it stalls 1 cycle. And flushed cells are dashes.

181
00:24:41,064 --> 00:24:51,859
Method card 4: the forwarding table and the stall table. For each source register, find its producer: the most recent older instruction that writes it.

182
00:24:52,309 --> 00:25:08,949
Compare when the value exists with when the consumer needs it. The table shows the result by distance, checked with the course simulator. For example, a load followed at once by an A L U use stalls 1 cycle, then uses W X.

183
00:25:09,399 --> 00:25:27,937
If a path exists in the same cycle, write one forwarding row: source, destination, register, from stage, to stage. The example of card 3 has two rows: W X of x 1 in cycle 5, and W M of x 3 in cycle 7.

184
00:25:28,387 --> 00:25:40,611
If there is no path, the consumer stalls in D, and the next instruction repeats F. Write one stall row for each: add stalls in D in cycle 3, the store in F.

185
00:25:41,061 --> 00:25:47,205
A stall moves all later instructions. So check the real cycles again, row by row.

186
00:25:47,655 --> 00:26:03,037
Common mistakes. A register file read in D is not a forward. Flushed instructions get no rows. And store data from 2 instructions ahead stalls 2 cycles, because only W M feeds store data.

187
00:26:03,487 --> 00:26:17,951
Method card 5: pipeline design questions. First, write what the new instruction does as a register transfer. The example: r d gets the memory word at r s 1 plus r s 2.

188
00:26:18,401 --> 00:26:28,086
Go stage by stage. F usually needs no change. D decodes the new opcode, reads the source registers, and makes the control bits.

189
00:26:28,536 --> 00:26:39,843
X, M and W: name the work in each stage. Here, the A L U adds r s 1 and r s 2, M reads memory, and W writes r d.

190
00:26:40,293 --> 00:26:45,498
New control bits ride down the pipeline registers, like Reg W En.

191
00:26:45,948 --> 00:26:54,609
Then check the hazards. When does the new value exist? Does it need a forwarding path, or a stall? Run a short program to check.

192
00:26:55,059 --> 00:27:01,545
If interrupts matter, change the state only at the commit point. Then the exception stays precise.

193
00:27:01,995 --> 00:27:17,888
Common mistakes. The old instructions need the new control bits too, set to 0. A new register, like HI or LO, needs its own hazard check. And give every stage, even when it has no change, and say why.

194
00:27:18,338 --> 00:27:28,855
Method card 6: branch prediction. Branches resolve in X. A wrong guess flushes the 2 younger instructions, so the penalty is 2 cycles.

195
00:27:29,305 --> 00:27:44,687
The CPI is 1, plus the branch fraction, times the wrong fraction, times the penalty. With 20 percent branches and 25 percent wrong: 1 plus point 2 times point 2 5 times 2, which is 1.1.

196
00:27:45,137 --> 00:27:54,971
For a table question, trace each run of the branch in order. Read the entry, predict, compare with the actual outcome, then update the entry.

197
00:27:55,421 --> 00:28:07,176
Use the state machine of the question. In a two-bit counter, taken moves one state right, and not taken one state left. States 1 0 and 1 1 predict taken.

198
00:28:07,626 --> 00:28:17,311
The B T B gives the target in F. Follow the rule of the question. In the spring 2020 quiz, only a taken branch writes it.

199
00:28:17,761 --> 00:28:27,148
In a pipeline diagram with no forwarding into the compare unit, the branch reads its operands in D, after the producer's W.

200
00:28:27,598 --> 00:28:36,942
Common mistakes. Predict first, then update. Start from the given state. And keep the flushed instructions in the diagram, as dashes.

201
00:28:37,392 --> 00:28:55,035
Method card 7: interrupts and exceptions. The pipeline finds exceptions in different stages. A bad P C in F, an illegal opcode in D, an overflow in X, a bad data address in M. So it finds them out of program order.

202
00:28:55,485 --> 00:29:03,293
Carry the flag and the P C of each instruction down to the commit point. On the slides, the commit point is M.

203
00:29:03,743 --> 00:29:18,591
Act only at the commit point. Write E P C and Cause. Kill the faulting instruction in M and the younger ones in X, D and F. The older one in W finishes. Then fetch the handler address.

204
00:29:19,041 --> 00:29:23,883
At the end of the handler, the return copies E P C back into the P C.

205
00:29:24,333 --> 00:29:35,448
In a design question, give the change in each stage, and why. Make every new state change at the commit point. Check nested interrupts and the younger instructions.

206
00:29:35,898 --> 00:29:46,586
Common mistakes. Do not act where the fault is found. Save the P C of the interrupted instruction. And check nested interrupts and the younger instructions.

207
00:29:47,036 --> 00:29:57,191
Method card 8: caches. Split the address. The offset has log 2 of the block size bits. 16 byte blocks give 4 offset bits.

208
00:29:57,641 --> 00:30:18,313
The index has log 2 of the number of sets. Sets are blocks over ways: 1024 over 16 is 64 blocks, and 64 over 2 ways is 32 sets, so 5 index bits. The tag is the rest: 32 minus 5 minus 4 is 23 bits.

209
00:30:18,763 --> 00:30:30,731
Lookup: hex 1 2 3 4 has offset 4, set 3, and tag 9. The index picks set 3. Compare the tag with every valid way. An equal tag is a hit.

210
00:30:31,181 --> 00:30:40,290
The average access time is t hit plus the miss rate times t miss. The miss time of a level is the average time of the next level.

211
00:30:40,740 --> 00:30:51,556
Work from the last level back. L 2: 10 plus point 2 times 100 is 30. L 1: 1 plus point 1 times 30 is 4 cycles.

212
00:30:52,006 --> 00:30:59,537
The CPI is the base CPI plus the misses per instruction times the miss penalty, for each cache.

213
00:30:59,987 --> 00:31:12,275
Common mistakes. Use the number of sets for the index, not the number of blocks. Keep byte addresses and block addresses apart. And in an empty cache, every first access misses.

214
00:31:12,725 --> 00:31:25,674
Method card 9: pseudo L R U. A 4-way set has 3 tree bits. B 0 is at the root, B 1 is over ways 0 and 1, and B 2 is over ways 2 and 3.

215
00:31:26,124 --> 00:31:33,036
A bit of 0 means the left side is L R U. A bit of 1 means the right side is L R U.

216
00:31:33,486 --> 00:31:44,537
On a miss, walk from the root and follow the bits. Here B 0 is 0, so go left. B 1 is 1, so go right. The victim is way 1, which holds B.

217
00:31:44,987 --> 00:31:47,312
Fill way 1 with the new block, E.

218
00:31:47,762 --> 00:32:00,221
Then every bit on the path from way 1 to the root points away from way 1. B 1 becomes 0, and B 0 becomes 1. B 2 is not on the path, so it stays 0.

219
00:32:00,671 --> 00:32:12,127
Common mistakes. Change only the path bits. Point them away from the used way. And P L R U is not true L R U: the victim can be a different way.

220
00:32:12,577 --> 00:32:30,646
Recap. Question 1. The CPI is a weighted sum. The memory CPI is 65, and the overall CPI is 28.2. In part C, 33.2 is more than 28.2, so program P does not benefit.

221
00:32:31,096 --> 00:32:45,709
Question 2 part A. A load-use stall, no forwarding into D or the compare unit, and a wrong guess that flushes 2 instructions. State your register file assumption: 15 or 14 cycles.

222
00:32:46,159 --> 00:32:59,834
Part B. blt runs 4 times. The entry goes 0 1, 1 0, 1 1, 1 1, and ends at 1 0. The B T B holds hex 10 after the first taken run.

223
00:33:00,284 --> 00:33:17,628
Question 3. Save the P C in E P C at the commit point. Save context is a copy and load context is a swap, at W. Flush after load context, and allow no nesting. Part C adds a lock bit to each cache line.

224
00:33:18,078 --> 00:33:28,745
For every question type, draw the picture first, then apply the steps of its method card. Good luck on the midterm. That is the end of video 17 c.
